Third-party assets
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The walking figure used by the demo apps — `apps/*/public/figure/
walking-figure.glb` — is "Business Man" from the Ultimate Modular Men Pack:

    By Quaternius (https://quaternius.com)
    Dedicated to the public domain under Creative Commons Zero v1.0
    https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
    Source: https://poly.pizza/m/JFrLIKqvCH

Unmodified, and carried here as a convenience rather than an obligation:
CC0 waives attribution, and this entry exists because saying where a file
came from is the honest thing to do, not because the licence asks for it.
The file holds twenty-four animation clips; the stage plays three of them
(walk, run, idle) and the rest ride along.

This asset belongs to the demo apps ONLY. It is not part of the `paperlab`
npm package, is not referenced by any stage preset in the library, and does
not appear in the published tarball — the library takes a `figure.model`
URL and the apps host the file. Anyone consuming the library brings their
own asset and their own licence obligations.



────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Hand and face tracking (dev-only harness)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

`apps/editor/hands.html` drives paper with a camera using MediaPipe Tasks:

    @mediapipe/tasks-vision — Copyright Google LLC
    Apache License 2.0
    https://github.com/google-ai-edge/mediapipe

The wasm runtime is that npm package's own, copied out of `node_modules` by
`pnpm hands:setup`. The two model files it also fetches —
`hand_landmarker.task` and `face_landmarker.task` — are Google's, from
storage.googleapis.com, and are pinned by digest in
`tools/hands-models.sha256`.

**Neither the runtime nor the weights are redistributed by this repository.**
They are not committed, they are not in the npm package, and they are not in
any build output: `hands.html` is a dev-only entry point, so `pnpm build`
emits neither the page nor its assets. Each developer's `pnpm hands:setup`
fetches them from Google directly.

That is worth knowing before anyone changes it. Making `hands.html` a public
page WOULD mean serving Google's model weights from this project's own
origin, which is redistribution, and the model cards' licence terms would
need reading first. The plumbing for it exists and is deliberately switched
off — see `handsAssets()` in `apps/editor/vite.config.ts`.
